        {
            "id": "Q1",
            "question": "What is the Air Quality Index (AQI)?",
            "answerable": true,
            "answer": "The Air Quality Index (AQI) is a tool for effective communication of air quality status to people in terms that are easy to understand. It transforms complex air quality data of various pollutants into a single number (index value), nomenclature and colour, and is based on ambient concentration values of air pollutants and their likely health impacts (health breakpoints). The AQI is calculated from sub\u2011indices for eight pollutants (PM10, PM2.5, NO2, SO2, CO, O3, NH3, and Pb), with the worst sub\u2011index determining the overall AQI. The index is divided into six categories: Good, Satisfactory, Moderately polluted, Poor, Very Poor, and Severe, each with defined concentration ranges and health impact descriptions. [1][3]",
