        {
            "id": "Q3",
            "question": "What is PM2.5?",
            "answerable": true,
            "answer": "PM2.5 is a pollutant whose ambient concentration is measured in micrograms per cubic meter (\u00b5g/m\u00b3). The AQI sub\u2011index for PM2.5 is calculated as a linear function of its concentration\u2014for example, a concentration of 31\u202f\u00b5g/m\u00b3 corresponds to a sub\u2011index of 51, 45\u202f\u00b5g/m\u00b3 to 75, and 60\u202f\u00b5g/m\u00b3 to 100. The AQI categories define health breakpoints for PM2.5: Good (0\u201350\u202f\u00b5g/m\u00b3), Satisfactory (51\u2013100\u202f\u00b5g/m\u00b3), Moderately Polluted (101\u2013200\u202f\u00b5g/m\u00b3), Poor (201\u2013300\u202f\u00b5g/m\u00b3), Very Poor (301\u2013400\u202f\u00b5g/m\u00b3), and Severe (401\u2013500\u202f\u00b5g/m\u00b3) [1].",
